# Story: somebody who cannot work with windows they cannot find tries the switch `outOfViewIsBehind`: what is not in the framed
# space stays where it is, behind what fills the screen, and every means of macOS's finds it there. A trial (QUESTIONS Q29, point 8; the board, 0.11).
# Reference: T52.
mac-tiling off
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail
open Terminal
set outOfViewIsBehind on
expect ok
dock 101 left
dock 103 beside 101 right
dock 102 beside 101 bottom
dock 104 beside 103 bottom
focus 101

# Filled: one window moves, once. The others are where they were, and count as out of view all the same.
zoom
expect fills 101
expect moved-once
expect still 102
expect still 103
expect still 104
expect in-front 101
expect focused 101

# And back: the same one window.
zoom
expect camera root
expect moved-once
expect still 102
expect still 103
expect still 104
expect visible 102
expect visible 103

# Switching to a window that is behind brings it into view, as it does when it is away (rule 4). Someone who lives framed stays framed.
zoom
focus 104
expect fills 104
expect in-front 104
expect focused 104
expect still 102
expect still 103

# Everything: whatever filled the screen goes back to its place, and nothing else is touched.
show-all
expect camera root
expect still 102
expect still 103
expect left-of 101 103
expect above 103 104

# Off again: out of view is at the corner, as Arbor ships.
set outOfViewIsBehind off
focus 101
zoom
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
expect hidden 104
